From: Yocto build fix
Subject: [PATCH] autoapp: show mouse cursor by default

This head unit has no touchscreen -- input is a plain USB mouse, whose
clicks InputDevice::handleTouchEvent() already translates into Android
Auto touch events (press/drag/release) transparently. But main()
starts the app with qApplication.setOverrideCursor(Qt::BlankCursor),
so the pointer is invisible from boot. Clicks still land correctly,
but the driver has no visual feedback for where the pointer actually
is, which reads as "clicking doesn't work" when it's really just
aiming blind.

There is a "Toggle Cursor" button on MainWindow that flips this, but
MainWindow is only reachable before a phone is connected -- once the
Android Auto video widget is up it covers MainWindow, so the toggle
can't be reached mid-session, and hitting an invisible button blind
on the home screen is not a reliable workaround either.

Default to Qt::ArrowCursor instead. The toggle button and its
click handler are untouched, so cursor can still be hidden again
for a touchscreen-equipped build where a visible pointer would be
undesirable.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [oe-specific: this build targets a
mouse-as-touch head unit with no touchscreen, where upstream's
blank-by-default assumption doesn't hold]

--- a/src/autoapp/autoapp.cpp
+++ b/src/autoapp/autoapp.cpp
@@ -102,7 +102,7 @@ int main(int argc, char* argv[])
     QObject::connect(&mainWindow, &autoapp::ui::MainWindow::openSettings, &settingsWindow, &autoapp::ui::SettingsWindow::showFullScreen);
     QObject::connect(&mainWindow, &autoapp::ui::MainWindow::openConnectDialog, &connectDialog, &autoapp::ui::ConnectDialog::exec);

-    qApplication.setOverrideCursor(Qt::BlankCursor);
+    qApplication.setOverrideCursor(Qt::ArrowCursor);
     QObject::connect(&mainWindow, &autoapp::ui::MainWindow::toggleCursor, [&qApplication]() {
         const auto cursor = qApplication.overrideCursor()->shape() == Qt::BlankCursor ? Qt::ArrowCursor : Qt::BlankCursor;
         qApplication.setOverrideCursor(cursor);
